<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Inner Asia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Inner Asian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Inner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> refers to the interior regions of Asia, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and the Central Asian steppe. It was the home of nomadic empires such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu Empire</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a> both bridged Chinese and Inner Asian political traditions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/inner-asia.jpg" alt="Inner Asia" width="320" height="172" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eric Thrift · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Inner_Asia_(SRI).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Inner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> refers to the interior regions covering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, as well as parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-russia">European Russia</a>, North Asia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>. It includes parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china">North China</a>, namely <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a>, and of Western China, namely <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, as well as southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>. The area overlaps with some, mostly historical, definitions of Central Asia, but certain regions often included in Inner Asia, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>, are not part of Central Asia by any definition. It may be regarded as the northern and western frontier of China proper and as bounded by East Asia proper, which consists of China proper, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>.</p><p>Its extent has been understood differently in different periods, and the term has a range of definitions. Morris Rossabi includes the five Central Asian countries, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Harvard's Committee on Inner Asian and Altaic Studies names Russian Turkestan, Xinjiang, eastern Iran, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, Afghanistan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, Qinghai, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> and north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>. The Mongolia and Inner Asia Studies Unit at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> defines it as an area centred on Mongolia and extending across the great steppes to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a>. Central Asia normally denotes its western part. Denis Sinor used the term in contrast to agricultural civilisations, and proposed Central Eurasia as an alternative, defining that region negatively as the part of Eurasia lying beyond the borders of the great sedentary civilisations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>By 1800 Chinese Inner Asia consisted of four main regions: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>. Many of these areas had been only recently conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> and during most of the Qing period were governed through administrative structures different from those of the older Chinese provinces. A Qing agency, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lifan-yuan">Lifan Yuan</a>, supervised them, and they were also known as Chinese Tartary. Historians of the Qing, such as those who compiled the New Qing History, often use the term for Qing interests outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> proper, though earlier dynasties such as the Han, Tang and Ming also extended their influence into Inner Asia.</p><p>Inner Asian studies in the West began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>. Hungarian explorers and scholars of the early 19th century travelled to Inner Asia to seek their own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> prehistory, the linguist Sándor Kőrösi Csoma, who later became a founder of Tibetology, being the first. Count Béla Széchenyi led an expedition in 1877–1880 and later founded the journal Turán, in whose masthead the term Inner Asian studies first appeared in 1913. In the first two decades of the 20th century the archaeologist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurel-stein">Aurel Stein</a> made important discoveries on four expeditions, and in 1928 published Innermost Asia. In 1940 Lajos Ligeti established the first academic chair for Inner Asian studies, at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Inner Asia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Dzungar Khanate was the last great independent nomadic empire of <b>Inner Asia</b>, and its contest with the Qing decided the political map of Central Eurasia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Inner Asian</b> territories such as Mongolia, Tibet and Xinjiang were managed separately through the Lifan Yuan, the Court of Colonial Affairs, under arrangements that preserved local rulers and religious hierarchies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>After the defeat of the Eastern Turks in 630 he was acclaimed 'Heavenly Khagan' by their chieftains, a recognition that the Tang empire straddled the Chinese and <b>Inner Asian</b> political worlds.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Tibetan Empire shaped the political map of <b>Inner Asia</b> for two centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tibetan-empire">Tibetan Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Modu's campaigns united the peoples of Mongolia and <b>Inner Asia</b>, destroyed and absorbed the Donghu confederacy to the east, defeated the Yuezhi of the Gansu region, and brought peoples further west under Xiongnu suzerainty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Black Death, which devastated Europe from 1347, very probably spread along the routes that connected the <b>Inner Asian</b> steppe, where plague was endemic among wild rodents, to the Black Sea and the Mediterranean.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
